;;; local.el.example -*- mode: emacs-lisp; lexical-binding: t; -*-

;; Reference for machine-local Doom settings.
;;
;; Copy this file next to it as `local.el' on any machine that needs to deviate
;; from the defaults:
;;
;;     cd ~/.config/doom && cp local.el.example local.el
;;
;; `local.el' is gitignored; this example is tracked. That split is the whole
;; point: machine- and employer-identifying details (logins, hostnames, paths
;; into internal repositories) belong in `local.el', never in the tracked
;; config, because this repository is public.
;;
;; `config.el' loads `local.el' immediately after defining the defaults below,
;; and skips it without complaint when absent. So a machine that needs no
;; overrides -- the personal one -- simply has no `local.el' and keeps every
;; default. Two consequences worth remembering when adding a variable here:
;;
;;   1. Give it a working default in `config.el' first. This file may not
;;      exist, so it can only ever adjust behaviour, not enable it.
;;   2. Use `setq', not `defvar'. `config.el' has already defined the variable
;;      by this point, and `defvar' would not overwrite the existing value.

;;; Variables

;; Non-nil marks this as the work machine, which selects GitHub Copilot as the
;; default agent shell (`SPC o l c') instead of Claude Code. `SPC o l C' still
;; opens the full picker on either machine.
;;
;; Defined in config.el, defaults to nil.
(setq my/work-machine-p t)

;;; local.el.example ends here
